WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.400
- Good morning, everyone.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.570
I'm Rohini Pande, I'm a
Professor of Economics

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.353
at Yale and Director of
the Economic Growth Center.

00:00:12.353 --> 00:00:14.504
It's my great pleasure to welcome all

00:00:14.504 --> 00:00:16.104
of you to this concluding event

00:00:16.104 --> 00:00:19.075
of a three day celebration
of the 65th anniversary

00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:22.133
of the Master's in International
Development Program.

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:24.685
And it's also part of
the 60th year celebration

00:00:24.685 --> 00:00:27.510
on the Economic Growth Center.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.899
So, when I started the

00:00:28.899 --> 00:00:31.351
as the Director of the
Growth Center last year,

00:00:31.351 --> 00:00:34.063
I spent some time just
going over the archives

00:00:34.063 --> 00:00:38.030
of Economic Growth Center and IDE Program.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.430
And what was striking to me was just what

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.120
a fertile period the 1960s was.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.860
I think more generally
for development economics

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.203
but especially at the Growth Center.

00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:52.220
You know, the 1960s marked the end

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.290
of colonialism in most parts of the world.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.729
And when a lot of open
questions about, you know,

00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:00.960
both how should low income
countries set their course

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.000
for the future, but also importantly

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.390
what were the roles and responsibilities

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:09.760
of academics and policymakers
in richer countries?

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.890
And these discussions were really mirrored

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.210
in the Economic Growth
Center and the researchers

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.870
who came through in the
student discussions.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.820
One of the first markers or
one of the first activities

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.040
of the Economic Growth Center

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.150
was the country studies program

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:28.250
with side faculty members
like Alejandro Carlos Diaz

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.500
to Latin America.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.100
And they found strong
networks in those countries

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.320
which continued after they came back.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.080
And I think as we look at the
Growth Center past, present,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.140
and future I think it's these links

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.690
with researchers across the globe.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.240
That is one of the things that at least

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.453
I believe EGC should be very proud of.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.680
Latin America was I think,
central to this in the 1960s,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.450
there was a lot of engagement
between what was happening

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.380
in Latin America with discussions
with economic development

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.257
more broadly and with the Growth Center.

00:02:02.257 --> 00:02:06.920
And so, we're delighted
to welcome Edmar Bacha,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.120
and Ana Cecilia Fieler
who will lead a discussion

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.270
on the Brazilian economy.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.780
So, let me start by
introducing both our panelists.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.670
So, Ana Cecilia Fieler is a
visiting Assistant Professor

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.680
at Economic Growth Center and
Yales Economics Department.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.260
She earned her PhD from
New York University,

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.490
and has worked at Princeton University,

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.670
and the University of Pennsylvania.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.740
In her research she combines
data and models to study

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.280
the effect of international trade

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.430
on labor markets and technology adoption

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.830
in developing countries

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:41.980
We are particularly
delighted to welcome her

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.850
because she will be
joining the IDE program,

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.950
and teaching macro economics in the fall.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.550
Professor Edmar Bacha graduated

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.650
from the Yale IDE Program in 1965.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.050
And he was the first student from

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.830
his original training program in Brazil

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.890
to be accepted in a doctoral program

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.160
after single year
post-graduate coursework.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.460
He continued on to Yale economics,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.440
and received his PhD in 1968.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.380
He's taught in several universities

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.530
both in the United States and Brazil.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.332
He's held positions as an economist

00:03:15.332 --> 00:03:17.260
in policy making organizations

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.920
like the United Nations
and the World Bank.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.540
And he's particularly
well known for being part

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.040
of a group of economists who came up

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.950
with a plan based on virtual currency

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.710
that succeeded in slowing down
Brazil's rampant inflation

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.420
in the 1990s.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.800
So, we're delighted that Professor Bacha

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.930
has joined us today and I
will hand it over to him now.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.780
He will give a presentation
for around 20 to 25 minutes,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.870
followed by a discussion
of first the presentation,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.620
and then a Q&amp;A with a Professor
Bacha by Cecilia Fieler.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.900
And then after that, I open for Q&amp;A.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.230
We delighted by the
audience that's joined us.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.950
We also realize that we
may not be able to get

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:04.030
to all Q&amp;A's, but Professor
Bacha has kindly agreed

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:05.870
that if as you write your questions

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.689
you could also provide your email address.

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:10.930
Then he would be very happy to respond

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.630
to them by email later.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.070
So, Professor Bacha, thank you again

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.320
for joining us and over to you.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.153
- Pleasure.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.380
Thank you very much.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:27.380
It's a pleasure to be back
to Hillhouse Avenue after

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.283
so many years even if it is only virtual,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:38.260
I'm only sorry that after this
we can not go out together

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:44.460
either to Pepe's or Sally's
for a beer and the pizza

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.303
but anyways, maybe next we will do that.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.170
My presentation today, you
know, it's based on my paper

00:04:55.370 --> 00:05:00.370
that we published in five years ago.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.240
It's a joint paper

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.240
with my dear late friend Regis Bonelli.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.817
And it was used for
call as I read it here,

00:05:13.817 --> 00:05:17.759
"Accounting for the rise
and fall of Brazil's growth

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:21.210
after world war two."

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:25.893
But given that the Brazil has
not improved actually has,

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.270
gotten worse, you know,
since we finished the paper

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:37.030
I decided to change the title
to something more dramatic

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:42.030
which is "What's gone wrong
with Brazil's economy."

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:43.920
And my presentation

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:48.813
it's story in four acts,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:53.120
I first are going to show
you, you know, one slide

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:59.040
how far behind the US,
Brazil has stayed over time.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.843
So, giving you a historical perspective.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.263
Then I focus more in detail

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:12.290
in the growth, collapse
of Brazil after 1980.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:17.840
Formulate the hypothesis that we had

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:22.360
in this paper that I
mentioned for this collapse,

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.510
and provide some arguments
for this hypothesis,

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:30.750
and then shift over to Ana Cecilia to see

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.960
what else she has to add.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.253
We start with this comparison with the US.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:43.800
In this slide, you know, we
see the ratio of Brazil's

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.640
to US per capita income,

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.640
all the way back from 1822, 2020.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.240
I divided this periods here

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:00.240
into six politically
important periods for Brazil.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:06.890
We, after we became
independent of Portugal in 1822

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.170
we had the constitutional
monarchy which lasted

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:16.440
for most of the 19th century.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.580
Then we decide to imitate
the United States,

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:25.580
and became presidentialist
with the first republic.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:30.770
We had the famous dictator
Getulio Vargas coming in

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:35.770
for 15 years, and then
after he was dismissed,

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.230
after the end of the second world war

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:42.350
we have this second Republic.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:46.530
The militaries took over again in 1964

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:51.480
from the a follower of Vargas

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.200
that was president at the time.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.490
They stayed on for 25 years.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:01.760
And since 1985, you know, we have we, I

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.470
we having a democracy,
even though we have all

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:07.643
these difficulties that we're having now

00:08:07.643 --> 00:08:10.070
with the current president.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:14.460
But anyways, in terms of this
per capita income comparisons

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.700
to the US you can see that, you know,

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.550
as Brazil became independent,

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.800
we the per capita income Brazil
was something like 60% of

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:24.843
the U S.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.270
Then by the end of the 19th century

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:33.320
this had gone down to 30%.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:38.000
It continued to decline
up until the 1920s,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.620
Brazil start growing fast, you know.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:44.110
United States entered the reset

00:08:46.996 --> 00:08:49.483
the depression in 1930,

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:55.570
but afterwards recovered very
nicely during the world war.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:59.210
So, that the beginning of the period

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.120
that I'm going to consider
in the following, you know,

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.142
1945 Brazil had the per capita income

00:09:06.142 --> 00:09:09.573
which was only 20% of the US.

00:09:10.735 --> 00:09:13.010
And then we started
catching period, you know,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:17.570
very hopeful that, so that by 1980,

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:19.987
Brazil's per capita income
had gone up, you know,

00:09:19.987 --> 00:09:24.987
a little bit above 40% of
the US but then, you know,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:28.597
and this is all that we're
going to talk about, you know,

00:09:28.597 --> 00:09:33.597
things have been going
downwards mostly all the time.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:38.510
And today we are only
at something like 23%

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.173
the per capita income of the US.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:47.560
Looking more specifically
at the post-war period

00:09:48.739 --> 00:09:51.170
here in this second slide,

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:55.290
I show you the Brazil's GDP growth rate

00:09:56.232 --> 00:09:59.493
from 1950 to 1920.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:05.610
In blue, the annual GDP growth rate,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.070
in orange, a 10 year moving average.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:15.020
As you can see from the 10
year moving average, you know,

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:17.863
all the way for actually
just from 1940, right?

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:22.593
Brazil was growing very
nicely, you know, over 7%.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:28.950
This was up until close to the 1980.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:33.950
There was a deceleration
around 75, but things along

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.480
if you look at the orange line, sorry,

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:48.830
there was a very deep
contraction of the economy.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.910
It continues to contract
up until the 1990.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:56.680
That was the last decade.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.160
And then, you know, up
and down a little bit

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.700
with the commodity cycles.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.860
And now, you know,

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:07.673
Brazil has really after
1940 tremendous bad periods

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:10.910
of which I'm not going

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.100
to talk much about, you know.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.740
And over the last 10 years actually

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:20.213
the Brazil GDP growth rate has been zero.

00:11:21.747 --> 00:11:25.210
So, but my point that
point to discuss, you know,

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:28.270
what happened, you know, in
Brazil was growing so nicely,

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:31.990
up until 1980 after the second
world war catching up to

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:36.990
the US, and then apparently
all of a sudden, you know,

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.410
if as a function of

00:11:42.853 --> 00:11:45.860
the debt crisis of the early eighties,

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:50.860
Brazil fell, and have never
more recovered, you know,

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:54.980
we grew seven and a half
and then start growing

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:56.030
at two and a half.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:12:01.030
You know, this is the focus
of the analysis that follows.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.690
And then in order to
analyze this, you know,

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:09.690
I use it very simple GDP growth
scheme, you know, I'd say.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:14.900
One factor of production growth scheme.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:19.910
It's GDP depends on the capital stock

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:23.430
in order to have capital stock to grow,

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.000
we need savings.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:28.140
And that's where the process here starts.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:30.810
You know, we start from savings,

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:34.290
and depending on what the
capital goods prices are

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:38.960
these savings motivate an increase

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:43.870
in this thought of capital
goods and given this increase

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:46.070
in this stock of capital goods,

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:49.380
depending on the productivity of capital

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:50.810
you'll get GDP growth.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:54.560
You know, this is basically
what I'm going to be showing

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:55.393
to you, you know,.

00:12:55.393 --> 00:12:58.870
So there are three
fundamental variables here

00:13:00.258 --> 00:13:03.030
to explain GDP growth.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:06.000
One is savings.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.750
The other is the price
of a new capital goods

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:13.900
that these savings are buying,
and then the productivity

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:18.900
of this capital goods in
order to generate GDP growth.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:22.530
So, there are three factors here

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:27.530
that explain the evolution of GDP growth.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.521
We start with savings.

00:13:30.521 --> 00:13:35.280
You know, this is evolution
of the saving rates by periods

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.710
from 1947 to 2020,

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:45.560
divided in eight periods

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.530
from 1947 to 2010 again.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:52.100
And that's, you can see here,
no I drove quick to focus

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:56.340
on total savings, the domestic savings

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:00.400
is the orange line
external savings are the

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:03.870
is the blue line, but I focus

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:06.840
on the dark line here
for the total savings.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:11.070
You see that, you know, during
this period up until 1980

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:13.963
was a period of increasing saving.

00:14:14.916 --> 00:14:19.916
And from 1980s, onward is the
period of decreasing savings.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:24.560
So, you can see in those
savings was a fact, you know,

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:28.120
in this slowing down of growth.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.480
But if you take an average,
you know, 47 to 80,

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:36.480
compared to 80-2020, the
difference, you know,

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.490
in terms of average is we looking at

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:43.140
these long historical periods,
the difference in average

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.940
is not very much, it's about
it's only 7%, you know.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:51.940
You couldn't explain the
drop of GDP growth from 7 1/2

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:56.190
to 2 1/2 on the basis of savings alone.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:59.390
You know, it contributed,
but it contributed.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:02.300
I go, I was going to argue,
you know, only marginally,

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:07.000
more important for what
happened to GDP growth

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:12.000
was the these factor which
is the capital goods prices.

00:15:12.095 --> 00:15:16.814
You know, the ratio of
investment prices to the prices

00:15:16.814 --> 00:15:18.647
of GDP, which is here.

00:15:20.248 --> 00:15:24.424
We have the showing this
capital good prices,

00:15:24.424 --> 00:15:29.174
and we have an index with the
price in 2000 equal to one.

00:15:32.801 --> 00:15:35.443
And as you can see, you know,

00:15:37.812 --> 00:15:38.940
I had to adjust the

00:15:39.876 --> 00:15:41.150
Regis and I had to adjust

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:44.180
to this period that have adjusted prices.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.560
Here is a period of hyperinflation.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.450
The National Council
went crazy too, you know.

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:53.320
So, we just forgot
about the original price

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:56.770
for the national accounts
and make an adjustment here.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:16:00.330
But anyways, it shows
quite clearly, you know,

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:05.330
which is a very unique
phenomenon in Brazil.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:07.830
You know, if you compared
you don't find this

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:09.590
in other countries, you know.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:14.590
A very substantial increase
in the price of capital goods

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:21.490
throughout dispute from the
1950s, up until the eighties.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:30.150
And this essentially, you
know, show that, you know,

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:34.890
if you compare now the
average price of capital goods

00:16:36.057 --> 00:16:40.893
in the period of 1980
to 2019, this is 30%,

00:16:43.126 --> 00:16:48.126
30% higher than it was in
the period previous to 1980.

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:56.350
So, that was a very substantial
part of the explanation

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:59.500
that even those savings
did not decline much,

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:04.500
the purchasing power of
savings over capital goods.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:07.870
That became very substantially,

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:10.560
not because you had less savings

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.530
but because the prices of capital goods

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:18.580
that the savings were buying
increased so substantially.

00:17:18.580 --> 00:17:23.220
The second phenomenon that
explained the growth collapse

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.210
is capital productivity
shown in this slide

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:31.050
from 1950 to 2020.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:36.050
This is GDP divided by
the stock of capital.

00:17:37.136 --> 00:17:40.120
And again, you can see,
you know, that throughout

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:45.120
this period up until 1980,
there was a continuous decline

00:17:45.296 --> 00:17:50.036
in the capital productivity in Brazil.

00:17:50.036 --> 00:17:54.190
And the average that we have

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:55.583
in the period,

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:02.417
after 1980, compared to
the period previous to 1980

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:06.777
is the decline of 25%, you know.

00:18:07.737 --> 00:18:12.077
So, you see, you know, capital
productivity declined 25%,

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:17.740
the price of capital goods increase 30%,

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:20.330
and savings declined by 7%.

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:23.540
And all this to get
put together, you know,

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:27.720
is what explain why GDP growth declined

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:32.870
from 7 1/2 to 2 1/2

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:36.810
When we compare pre to post 1980.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:40.920
That's the fundamental
explanation that we elaborate

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:45.920
in the paper that I previously mentioned.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:49.510
So, my conclusion here, tentative

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:53.040
because I'm going to try to
substantiate in the following

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:58.040
is that the collapse in
GDP growth since 1980

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:03.830
had more to do with lower
capital productivity,

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:09.000
and higher capital goods
prices than to reduce savings.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.600
Hypothesis is that these two
phenomenon are interconnected.

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:18.520
They are a consequence
of Brazil maintaining

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:21.843
its economy closed to international trade.

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:28.530
Brazil deepened an inefficient
import substitution process.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:34.530
Instead of promoting
exports as the countries

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:39.530
